00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.160
What if a factory could adapt the moment

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:08.559
conditions change?

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.360
Manufacturing systems consist of

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:13.840
interconnected machines, resources, and

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.480
processes that harmonically transform

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.400
raw materials into finished products.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.640
The performances of a manufacturing

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.119
system are often pertinent to the rate

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.199
of throughputs, the cost of production,

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:27.439
the quality of finished products, the

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:29.359
resilience to disruptions, or the life

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:31.119
cycle sustainability.

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:33.520
But real factories still face

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:36.000
disruptions such as machine failures,

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.719
material shortages, labor challenges,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.960
demand variation, and tooling problems.

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:43.040
When a disruption occurs, production

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.680
performance can degrade, and the system

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.079
may miss target KPI.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.079
New technologies like digital twin have

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:52.559
been increasingly deployed to address

00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:54.800
these challenges. A digital twin

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:57.199
provides a digital representation of the

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.760
manufacturing system for data streaming,

00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:02.719
visualization, simulation, process

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.559
control, and optimization. Digital twin

00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:06.479
is powerful, but there is still a

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:08.240
challenge in complex manufacturing

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:10.560
systems. The number of possible

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.880
configurations and disruption scenarios

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.040
can grow rapidly. Traditional redesign

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.280
and simulationbased search can become

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.159
timeconuming and computationally

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.799
expensive. This research presents a new

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:24.880
generative AI framework for on-fly

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:26.799
manufacturing system design under

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.960
disruption. The goal is to generate

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:31.920
adaptive system designs that align

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.320
production performance with target KPI.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.280
The framework has three main components,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.360
a predictor, a generator, and a critic.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.759
Together, they learn relationships among

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.119
machine availability, design decisions,

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:47.840
and KPI outcomes. As the environment of

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:50.159
a manufacturing job shop is dynamically

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.560
evolving, plant managers are provided

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:54.640
with this generative AI tool that

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.040
generates candidate designs and

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:00.000
implementation costs by taking a

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.399
specific set of input variables. When

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.880
disruptions occur to machines, material

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:07.280
handling systems, job orders, or raw

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.360
materials, plant managers can feed new

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.520
input variables to this tool and

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.400
generate reconfigurable designs for

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.480
evaluation and assessment. Similarly,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.879
when there is no disruption or the

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.440
system is restored to conditions before

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:24.720
disruptions, plant managers can also

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.879
input a different set of KPI targets or

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.800
job order variations to generate

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:31.280
candidate designs for continuous

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.519
improvements. In this vision, the

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.400
physical factory, digital twin, and

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:38.080
generative AI work together. The factory

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.319
provides the real manufacturing

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.160
environment. The digital twin provides

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:44.319
simulation intelligence and the

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:47.040
generative AI framework supports

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:49.280
redesign decisions. This work shows how

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.360
generative AI can support resilient

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.440
manufacturing by helping factories

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.400
respond faster to disruptions and

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.400
changing performance goals.


